  "statement": "The real numbers $x$ and $y$ satisfy $x^{3}-3xy^{2}=m$ and $3x^{2}y-y^{3}=n$. By considering the complex number $z=x+iy$, show that $z^{3}=m+in$, and deduce that $x^{2}+y^{2}=(m^{2}+n^{2})^{2/3}$.",
  "solution": "We have $z^{3}=(x+iy)^{3}=x^{3}+3ix^{2}y-3xy^{2}-iy^{3}=(x^{3}-3xy^{2})+i(3x^{2}y-y^{3})=m+in$. Taking moduli, $|z|^{3}=|z^{3}|=|m+in|=\\sqrt{m^{2}+n^{2}}$. Since $|z|^{2}=x^{2}+y^{2}$, we have $(x^{2}+y^{2})^{3/2}=\\sqrt{m^{2}+n^{2}}$, so $x^{2}+y^{2}=(m^{2}+n^{2})^{2/3}$.",
